      "document_classification": "HUMAN_ONLY",
      "inputText": "I could probably turn this into a longer personal account, but I really just wanted to share this experience.\nFor years, I was working on this really ambitious sci-fi/fantasy project. I guess I still am; I definitely want to finish it. But I wasn't able to write regularly on it. The writer's block was constant, perhaps because of the pressure I was putting on myself.\nI solved this problem by accident. I played a video game (League of Legends) with truly derpy lore that nonetheless had a bit of cartoony charm to it. I started a community for roleplaying and themed matches, and I wrote some filler fanfic/fanlore/whatever to add flavor to the competitions. People enjoyed it, so I kept writing. And it was easy, because my attitude to start with was, \"whatever, just write something, it doesn't have to be perfect, it's just for a silly game.\"\nBut the thing is, over time, as I kept writing this silly throwaway lore, I found myself striving almost unconsciously to make it good. Honestly, it wasn't until over a year or so into it that I recall realizing that I was writing now. I suppose it was all very Zen: I only succeeded in my endeavor to begin writing regularly when I \"gave up\" on it.\nI realize, of course, that this is just a start. If I want to become a \"real\" author, I need to move beyond writing entertaining little fanfic stories. But after years of procrastination and angst, I'm very pleased that I've at least begun that journey in earnest now. And I think I've learned more in a couple of years of this than I did in 5+ years of angsting over \"my masterpiece\". Like so many real authors have said, you've just got to write.\ntl;dr: If you're one of those people who can't get past page one, try starting a new project that you don't really \"care\" about, with no expectation of producing a masterpiece, and just write \"garbage\". You might be surprised at how well you progress with the pressure gone.\n",
      "document_id": null
    }
